WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:06.660
Much like game of thrones before it, HBO's Westworld is an elaborate,

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.660
epic puzzle box of a production that serves in the end as an excuse to explore something much smaller

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.480
and much more important: Character.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.400
This is why despite the sweeping vistas, what holds everything else up are the conversations,

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:27.420
how they're written and how they're acted. Today, I want to take a look at one conversation featuring the great Anthony Hopkins.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:34.880
Specifically, I want to examine how Hopkins acts the scene, how he elevates every line of dialogue to a hypnotically interesting level.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.440
You imagine I've gone mad,

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:39.850
do you?

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.340
Lost my way?

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.560
We're simply concerned about the extent of the changes you're making.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.250
We want to protect your legacy.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:49.210
My legacy?

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:56.400
Okay, the background of the scene is that Theresa Cullen, an operations manager of the Westworld theme park is trying to stop -

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.480
or at least stall - the creation of Robert Ford, Anthony Hopkins' new storyline for the guests,

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:06.300
a storyline that is generally wreaking chaos on things to the point of worrying the parks financiers.

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:09.900
It's clear that Theresa feels that Ford's undertaking is just too risky,

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:14.740
but instead of forthrightly telling him so, she puts things in euphemistic terms.

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:17.100
We want to protect your legacy.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:17.800
My legacy?

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:32.340
Okay, that pause is fully 7 seconds long and it is amazing. After reacting in disbelief at such a shameless euphemism,

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.160
Hopkins, in silence, cycles through a few emotions.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:42.180
First, disbelief. Then a consideration of the words for a split second at their face value.

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:47.250
Maybe he should safeguard his legacy. Maybe he shouldn't be so ambitious at his age.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:53.740
Then a smile and the scene will feature several shades of smile this one is kind,

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:59.000
as kind as can be in the face of what he considers to be an attack, one that insults his intelligence.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.400
As he looks down and chuckles, the smile seems to acquire some suppressed anger.

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:12.419
He can only bear to look at her in quick glances as he composes himself, then he fires back an antagonistic remark.

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.389
You don't like this place very much. Do you?

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:18.509
I've seen many of you come and go over the years.

00:02:18.509 --> 00:02:23.180
And I can tell the ones who enjoy the time here the ones who don't.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.820
What's impressive here is how Hopkins breaks down the dialogue as written.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:34.680
In interviews, Hopkins has described how he reads scripts 200 times or so to the point where the dialogue is lodged in his brain,

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:40.798
then he steps away, lets it gestate in his mind, and when he arrives on set he relaxes himself and

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:43.059
"let the part play through you."

00:02:43.059 --> 00:02:45.179
By having such a command over the words,

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:50.216
Hopkins is able to modulate the tempo of their delivery to get the most out of it.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:53.100
This sentence, for example, is sped through.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:58.360
"I've seen many of you come and go over the years." As is the first part of the next sentence until a pause after "who"

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:03.880
drawing out the word "enjoy" which falls right back into a speedy delivery almost slipping over the words,

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:09.870
until the final two which, coupled with eye contact, slow down to function as an accusation.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:17.320
I've seen many of you come and go over the years. And I can tell the ones who enjoy the time here the ones who don't.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:19.700
I admire it.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:21.320
The audacity of it.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:23.220
Oh...

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.090
A small interjection.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:31.950
But see how he opens up like a flower from the hunched position, how he releases a little tension to receive the compliment. Great touch.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:36.630
I came here once as a child with my parents. I think we even sat at this table.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:43.220
When I started working here, I realized this place wasn't something I would enjoy.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:43.720
Ah!

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:50.520
And then we get a second interjection when his suspicions are confirmed followed by hunching again, and a deflated neck.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:54.000
Then, from this position, Hopkins launches into his key speech.

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:58.109
In the beginning I imagined things would be

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:01.410
perfectly balanced.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:05.360
Even had a bet with my partner, Arnold, to that effect

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:11.160
He made a hundred hopeful storylines. Of course, almost no one took us up on them. I lost the bet.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:18.179
Arnold always held a somewhat dim view of people. He preferred the hosts.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.100
He begged me not to let you people in. The money men.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.080
Delos.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.300
Okay, we're about to learn that this whole meeting is a threat set up by Hopkins.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:34.920
And to signal that, Hopkins incrementally dials up the menace across three terms that specify who he's threatening.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:37.580
"You people." "Money men." "Delos."

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.280
Listen to it again. He begged me not to let you people in.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.290
The money men.

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:43.700
Delos.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.899
I told him it'd be fine. That you didn't understand what you were paying for.

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:49.959
It's not a business venture, not a theme park,

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:53.380
but an entire world.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:56.740
We designed every inch of it,

00:04:59.499 --> 00:05:01.499
every blade of grass.

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:06.850
In here we were gods,

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.680
and you

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.820
were merely our guests.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:18.469
Watching this the first time it was here that it struck me. How crucial Ford's personality is to the functioning of the series.

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:24.600
I mean, Westworld is, in many ways, a ridiculous idea both as a theme park and as a TV show.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:33.140
To work, the conceit had to be mapped, reflected, justified in the complex desires and ambitions of its creators.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:34.960
The writers have achieved that in Ford,

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:38.095
But it's Hopkins who really brings these things to life.

00:05:38.095 --> 00:05:40.160
And how did that work out for Arnold?

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:42.450
Oh, sadly,

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.450
he lost his perspective.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:46.790
He went Mad.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:52.669
I haven't, as you well know, I have always seen things

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:55.530
very clearly.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:06:00.940
The threat now explicit, Hopkins colors the rest of the scene with a sinister glee.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.500
This is the table I sat at with my parents.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:09.340
We know everything about our guests, don't we? As we know everything about our employees.

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:15.160
From here on out he presses his advantage, not with force, but with comfort, with a smile.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:18.320
No longer needing to convince Teresa of his dominance,

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:24.040
Hopkins dances into different attitudes for a couple of seconds before falling back into that smile.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.631
Like right here, where he's matter-of-fact.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:31.400
We know everything about our guests, don't we? As we know everything about our employees.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:33.439
Or here where he's concerned.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:36.720
I do hope you will be careful with Bernard.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:42.280
Or here where he's genuinely caring as his eyes wander off for a moment too.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:45.940
He has a- a sensitive disposition.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:51.389
The producers of this show are smart to keep the camera fixed on Hopkins' face.

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:58.409
His ability, learned across many decades, to convey such a web of feeling and suggest such a

00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:02.580
universe of feeling within about a character we know little about

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:08.939
expands the entire show's range and strengthens its persuasive power as a piece of art.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.240
That's what a great actor can do.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:19.320
He can give words and images or a short scene like this a volcanic energy with a quiet look.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:26.440
In Anthony Hopkins' hands, in his eyes, Westworld isn't just a reflection of its creators personality,

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:32.437
It's a self-portrait. You can tell the board that my narrative will be completed on time,

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:37.620
and it won't be a retrospective as I'm sure you have all feared.

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:47.760
I'm not the sentimental type.

